 ( { Black is a pawn up, but hanging on to it with } 1. -- h5 $6 { makes little sense in view of } 2. Qg5 { With his next move, Taimanov instead bets on his main asset, the passed d-pawn. } ) 
 { -KEY- } d4 $1 2. Qxh6 Qe5 $1 { An accurate choice, in order to limit the enemy queen and enable ...f4-f3+. } ( { The immediate } 2... d3 $2 { was premature because of } 3. Qg5 { with a perpetual. } ) 
3. Qh4 d3 $1 ( { Of course not } 3... f3 $4 4. Qg3 $1 { and White even wins. The well-calculated text-move instead aims at deflecting the enemy queen with the d-pawn so that the f-pawn can advance to the second rank. } ) 
4. Qd8+ Kg7 5. Qxd3 f3+ 6. Kg1 ( { Unfortunately for White, } 6. g3 { runs into } Qe2 $1 { - Taimanov. } ) 
Qe1+ 7. Kh2 ( 7. Qf1 { fails to } f2 { - Taimanov. } ) 
f2 { From now on, Black's only concern is to hide his king from the queen checks. } 8. Qg3+ Kf8 ( 8... Kf6 { is also possible, as observed by codydegen. After, for instance, } 9. Qd6 Kf5 { Black proceeds in similar way as in the main line, sending the king to the opposite corner of the board. } ) 
9. Qb8+ Ke7 $1 { The king gets ready to cross the board in search of shelter. We are already familiar with this idea from Examples 49 and 50 (Krush-Akopian and Marshall-Tarrasch) in Chapter One. } 10. Qc7+ Ke6 11. Qc4+ Ke5 { Seeing that the black king will soon hide on b1, White resigned. } ( { There could have followed } 11... Ke5 12. Qc5 Ke4 13. Qc4 Ke3 14. Qc5 Kd3 15. Qc4 Kd2 16. Qd4 Kc1 17. Qf4 Kb2 18. Qd4 Kxa2 19. Qa4 Kb1 { and wins - Taimanov. } ) 

