 { White could take a pawn on f4, but I soon found a more tempting possibility, particularly in a rapidplay game. } 1. { -KEY- } d5 $1 { Transposing to a queen ending with a far-advanced passed pawn. } ( { On its part Rybka prefers } 1. dxc5 { seeing that } Rxe5 { runs into } 2. Rxe5 Rxe5 3. Qd8 Kf7 4. Qc7 Kf6 5. Qd6 Re6 6. Qf8 Kg5 7. h4 $1 Kxh4 8. Qh6 Kg3 9. Qh2 { Well, for a simple human plagued by time-trouble, I don't think my solution is bad either. } ) 
Rxe5 2. Rxe5 Rxe5 3. Rxe5 Qxe5 4. d6 Qe8 5. d7 { A passed pawn on the seventh rank is a huge asset in any queen ending, and Black now faces a difficult task. By the way, note that if the queens were rooks then the picture would change completely. } Qd8 6. Qd5+ Kf8 ( 6... Kg7 7. Qxb7 Kf6 { was more tenacious, when White could consider the prophylactic } 8. h3 $5 { before taking the c-pawns with his queen. } ) 
7. Qxc5+ Kf7 8. Qxc4+ Ke7 9. Qe4+ $1 ( { Not } 9. Qxf4 $6 Qb6 { followed by 10...Qxb2. } ) 
Kxd7 10. Qxb7+ Ke6 { Unfortunately for Black he can't swap queens because of his pawn minus, thus he soon loses another one. } 11. Qe4+ Kf6 12. Qxf4+ Kg7 13. Qe5+ { Centralization. The rest is easy. } Kg8 14. h3 Qb6+ 15. Kh2 h5 16. f4 $1 { Simplest, in order to expose Black's king. } Qf2 17. f5 $1 g5 { Both captures on f5 led to a lost pawn ending. } 18. Qe8+ Kg7 19. Qe7+ Kh6 20. Qf6+ { In view of Qf7xh5 next, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

