 { The strength of a passed pawn in queen endings can also be exploited by the defender, as in the next example.Things look difficult for Black, who is not just a pawn down but also suffers from an exposed king. And how to cope with the threat at g6? } 1... { -KEY- } b4 $1 { Betting on the passed pawn. } ( { The passive } 1... Qh6 $2 { led to instant defeat after } 2. Bd5 Kh7 3. Qe7 ) 
2. Bxg6 Bxg6 3. Qxg6+ Kh8 4. Qf6+ Kh7 { Black's idea is clear: the b-pawn will deflect the enemy queen from the kingside defence. } 5. g4 ( { a) If } 5. Kg2 { then } Qd5 { followed by 6...b3. } ) 
 ( { b) Whereas } 5. h5 b3 $1 6. Qf7 Kh8 7. Qxb3 Qxf2 { is a theoretical draw - Golubev. There could follow } 8. Kh3 Qf1 9. Kg4 Qe2 10. Qf3 Qe6 11. Qf5 Qe3 { and White can't easily progress. Compared with the similar ending in the final part ofExample 311 (Larsen-Keres), here the stronger side is suffering from the fact that one his pawns is a rook's pawn, which means that his king has fewer chances to hide from the enemy checks. Furthermore, stalemate scenarios are enabled. } ) 
b3 6. g5 ( { Or } 6. Qf7 Kh8 7. Qxb3 Qxf2 8. Kh3 Qf1 9. Kg3 Qe1 { etc. } ) 
b2 7. Qf7+ ( { After } 7. g6 Kh6 8. g7 Kh7 9. Qf8 Qxf2 $1 10. Qxf2 b1=Q $11 { Black draws. } ) 
Kh8 8. g6 Qh6 $1 { Covering everything. } 9. Qf6+ Kg8 10. Qe6+ ( { In the event of } 10. Qxb2 { one simple path to the draw is } Qf4 { with a perpetual along g4-d1. } ) 
Kg7 ( { But not } 10... Kh8 $2 11. Qe5 Kg8 12. Qb8 $1 { when } Kg7 13. Qxb2 Kxg6 { invites } 14. Qb6 { , whereas } Qf8 { runs into a new intermediate check: } 13. Qb3 $1 Kg7 14. Qxb2 Kxg6 15. Qd2 { or } 15. Qd4 { , when two minus pawns are one too much for Black. } ) 
11. Qf7+ 
 1/2-1/2

