 { Black has just played ...Qe7-c5, with some activity for the pawn. Gelfand makes a technical choice. } 1. { -KEY- } Re2 $1 { Giving up the e-pawn in order to swap rooks and reach an endgame with queens and minor pieces, where the passed a-pawn will become a huge asset. } ( { Other options are less clear; e.g. } 1. Re3 Nd5 2. Qe8 Kh7 3. Rf3 Qc1 $1 { with the threat of ...Rb1. } ) 
Rxe2 2. Bxe2 Qxe5 3. Bf3 g5 $1 4. h3 $1 ( { Correctly preventing the ...g5-g4 pin-prick, which could come in handy for Black on different occasions; e.g. } 4. Kg2 g4 5. Bxg4 Nxg4 6. Qxg4 Qd5 ) 
g6 $2 { Very slow. } ( { Akopian must have underestimated White's reply, otherwise he would have preferred the active } 4... Qe1 5. Kg2 e5 $1 { intending ...e5-e4 - Krasenkow. At this point, White could choose between } 6. Bd1 { heading for c2, } ( { and } 5... g6 5. Bd5 $5 Nxd5 7. Qa8 Kh7 8. Qxd5 { in both cases keeping some winning chances. } ) 
 ) 
5. Qa7 $1 { A key move - the queen clears the way for the a-pawn, while keeping an eye on the opposite flank. Such a method is typical in pure queen endings, and we saw a related case in Example 55 (Larsen-Andersson). Curiously, Black can't do much to prevent the enemy plan. } Kg7 ( { Again } 5... Qe1 { preparing ... e5-e4, seemed more active, although after } 6. Kh2 $1 e5 7. a4 { , things don't change much compared to the further course of the game - Krasenkow. } ) 
6. a4 
 1-0

